Astropy currently provides a cache system for keeping track of the URL from which external data was downloaded, as well as its location on the user's local disk. It is not uncommon in astronomy for this external data to be quite large, and to require reduction before it is suitable for day-to-day use. This Issue is a request for astropy to provide bookkeeping tools for managing the data reductions. 

Without such bookkeeping tools, astropy users typically keep their reduced catalogs stored somewhere on their local disks, and have to "manually remember" the URL from which the catalogs are derived, as well as the various cuts that were applied in the reduction. However, it would be better practice for everyone if the reduced catalogs automatically had metadata attached to them containing the original URL, a frozen copy of the exact code used to reduce the original catalog, as well as various notes the astropy user may find helpful. This way, everyone's reduced catalogs are self-expressive. Bonus behavior would be if the catalogs may be over-written by further uses of the astropy data reduction tools, but only after issuing a warning, and/or optionally changing the "version" of the reduced catalog. 

