@astrofrog [suggested](https://github.com/astropy/pyregion/pull/54) to raise this issue with `astropy`. I believe that currently `celestial_pixel_scale()` returns the **arithmetic mean** of scales for each axis:  `scale=1/2*(scale_X+scale_Y)`.

However, current definition is not related in any way to other important quantities such as pixel area. If there are no strong reasons to keep this definition, e.g., if `astropy` does not try to mimic the behavior of some other package, I would like to propose to use a definition for the "average" scale such that `scale**2=pixel area`. This definition works for both square and non-square pixels. Of course this is just one possible definition and other choices may be possible, so it may be worth to discuss this in order to obtain more suggestions.

If proposed definition is adopted, then for non-square (**but orthogonal**) axes, one could use the **geometric mean** `scale=sqrt(scale_X*scale_Y)` - a formula used in the old `pyregion` package. For non-square **and** non-orthogonal axes, I would suggest using `scale=sqrt(abs(det(CD)))` as explained in https://github.com/astropy/pyregion/pull/54 This latest formula is correct (I think) for square and non-square pixels (orthogonal and non-orthogonal) such that `scale**2=pixel area`.

