@hamogu - I am trying to use ascii.read() to reduce a large (~6Gb) ASCII dataset into a processed binary file in which I have applied some cuts. The strategy I have been taking is to the use data_start and data_end keywords in a loop over chunks of the file, so that only ~100Mb chunks are loaded into memory at a time. Then I make cuts on the chunk, append a Table with the rows passing the cuts, and move on to the next chunk. So, pretty classic 'big data' task. 

What I am finding is that ascii.read() is failing to only load chunks at a time. In particular, _any_ call to my ASCII file with ascii.read(fname, data_start=i, data_end=i+chunksize) results in an interminable stall of the python interpreter. So far as I am able to tell, I have no way to use astropy's ascii reader to process data this large. I have verified that this behavior does not happen for an artificially reduced version of the file (~100Mb) with an otherwise identical format. 

I understand that ascii.read() has been re-vamped for v1 with 'big data' tasks like this in mind. However, I have verified that this stall-out occurs both with v0.4.x and also with the version of the master branch that is current at the time of this issue.

